      "title": "12 killed, over 300 injured in latest attack on Saudi's Riyadh airport",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/12-killed-over-300-injured-in-latest-attack-on-saudi-s-riyadh-airport-126101100047_1.html",
      "description": "The latest attack on the international airport in Saudi Arabia's capital of Riyadh killed 12 people and injured over 300 others, civil aviation authorities said early Sunday, with some in critical condition. A Saudi-led coalition blamed Iran-backed Houthi rebels in neighboring Yemen.\nIt was the deadliest attack in any Gulf Arab country since the start of the Iran war, whose fronts have expanded to include the recent surge in fighting between the Houthis and Saudi-backed Yemeni government forces.\nA missile struck a King Khalid International Airport complex housing terminals 3 and 4 for domestic flights, a regional official said, speaking on condition of anonymity because they weren't authorized to speak to the media.\nThe Saudi statement said the dead included four Saudi citizens as well as two Bangladeshi nationals, a Palestinian, a US citizen, an Egyptian, a Jordanian, a Syrian and a Sudanese. It said the injured were being treated at several hospitals.\nTrump said he would consider",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-11T01:40:47"
    },
    {
      "title": "US 'may' join Saudi strikes on Houthis after Riyadh airport attack: Trump",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/us-may-join-saudi-strikes-on-houthis-after-riyadh-airport-attack-trump-126101000984_1.html",
      "description": "President Donald Trump on Saturday said he is evaluating whether the US should join Saudi strikes on the Houthis.\n\"We may. We're going to look at it,\" he told reporters here.\n\"We just found out about the recent attack. So we'll make a decision. We move very quickly,\" he said.\nThe airport in Saudi Arabia's capital, Riyadh, was attacked again Saturday and evacuated, two regional officials said, just days after three Saudi citizens, including a pilot, were killed as the kingdom faces an extraordinary series of assaults in the new front in the Iran war.\nA missile struck a complex housing terminals 3 and 4 for domestic flights at King Khalid International Airport and many people were wounded, one of the officials said.\nThe airport has been targeted multiple times in the week since a Saudi-led coalition launched a major military offensive against Iran-backed Houthi rebels in neighbouring Yemen.\nThe Houthis claimed responsibility for the earlier attacks, including one Wednesday that kil",
